s:\n- Capsid library: say 12 engineered AAV variants (AAV2, AAV8, AAV9, AAVrh.10, PHP.B, PHP.eB, AAV-X1, AAV9.47, CAP-Mac, AAV-LK03, AAVhu.37, AAV3B) — DESIGN choice\n- Receptor panel: 6 receptors (given)\n- Pairs: 12 × 6 = 72 pairs\n- Per pair: N poses (e.g., 8 docking poses × 5 AF-Multimer seeds = 40 models?) — need to keep under $1,000.\n\nCompute budget allocation with $1,000:\n- Phase 1 (pilot): measure AF-Multimer per-complex runtime on H100, docking runtime, MD runtime\n- Phase 2 (enumerate): run the grid\n- Phase 3 (MD refine top poses)\n- Phase 4 (motif analysis: 3-fold axis engagement geometry)\n\nLet me think about the compute axis. \"compute_axis.quantity\" — the quantity this test varies that more parallel compute buys. If restructuring.method is not none: the chain length or the number of runs the answer needs. Here, sequential is \"none\" per the option record — every pair is independent. But the schema wants restructuring.sequential_part and method; method can be \"none\". If method is not none, I need condition, condition_phase, baseline_sequential, baseline_copies. If method is none, those optional fields can be omitted... but wait: \"If method is not none, also give condition...\" — so if method is \"none\", those fields are optional and can be omitted. But the schema for restructuring requires only sequential_part and method. Good.\n\nBut wait — the prompt says \"In restructuring, sequential_part is the part of this computation that one answer waits on step after step, and how many steps; method is why those steps need not run in order and how your plan computes them at the same time instead, or \"none\" if the plan only runs independent work side by side or splits each step across GPUs.\"\n\nActually for this option, the option record says sequential is \"none: every capsid–receptor pair is an independent docking or AlphaFold-Multimer job with its own random seeds\". So method = \"none\" seems right — the plan only runs independent work side by side. Then compute_axis.quantity would be \"the quantity this test varies that more parallel compute buys\" — e.g., number of capsid–receptor–pose model runs computed. Unit: \"model runs\" or \"complexes modeled\". Levels: e.g., 24, 72, 288 (pairs × seeds) or something like that.\n\nHmm, but there's also: \"compute_axis.quantity: The quantity this test varies that more parallel compute buys. If restructuring.method is not none: the chain length or the number of runs the answer needs.\" Since method is \"none\", quantity is just the quantity varied — number of capsid–receptor pairs enumerated (or model runs).\n\nPredictions.if_lever and if_not: \"the outcomes you expect, on the same measured quantity, if more compute does or does not change the answer.\" The measured quantity here: fraction of transcytosis-competent pairs that share the 3-fold-axis GPI-engagement motif vs. the number of model runs/pairs enumerated. Hmm.\n\nLet me think about what the actual measured outcome is. The plan enumerates capsid-receptor complexes and tests whether transcytosis-competent pairs share a geometric motif (e.g., 3-fold axis engagement of GPI-anchored receptors). The measured outcome could be: \"number/fraction of known transcytosis-competent pairs whose top-scoring interface places receptor at the 3-fold axis\" and how this converges as we add more pose sampling (more compute).\n\nif_lever: with more compute (more poses/seeds per pair), the motif classification stabilizes/converges — e.g., ≥5/6 of transcytosis-competent pairs classified as 3-fold-axis-engaging with motif assignment stable across seeds, and the separation from non-transcytosing pairs holds.\nif_not: more poses don't change the classification — motif fraction unchanged within noise between compute levels, meaning the answer is limited by structural accuracy not sampling.\n\nActually, better framing: the compute axis is \"number of capsid–receptor–pose model runs\". More compute buys more exhaustive sampling of binding modes. The question is whether exhaustive sampling surfaces a shared motif or shows none exists.\n\nMeasured quantity: the number of independent transcytosis-competent pairs classified into the 3-fold-axis engagement class (or the gap in interface-geometry features between transcytosis-competent and intracellular-retained pairs).\n\nDecision rule: e.g., if at the highest level the motif-assignment rate for competent pairs exceeds that for retained pairs by ≥30 percentage points and is stable (same assignment under 2× sampling, Cohen's κ ≥ 0.8), call if_lever... Hmm, but if_lever and if_not are about whether MORE COMPUTE changes the answer, not about whether the motif exists.\n\nLet me re-read: \"predictions.if_lever and predictions.if_not are the outcomes you expect, on the same measured quantity, if more compute does or does not change the answer.\"\n\nSo the \"lever\" is more parallel compute. The measured quantity is what we measure. if_lever: outcome if compute changes the answer. if_not: outcome if compute doesn't change the answer.\n\nExample: measured quantity = \"fraction of pairs whose top binding-mode class (3-fold vs2-fold vs other) is stable across seeds\" or \"the motif separation score between competent and retained pairs\".\n\nif_lever: as runs go from 72 to 576, the motif-separation score rises above threshold (e.g., ≥0.30) and the catalog of candidate signatures changes — more compute surfaces the motif.\nif_not: the score stays within noise of the 72-run value at all levels — exhaustive enumeration confirms or closes the motif without compute mattering.\n\nHmm, actually I think the cleaner framing: the answer to the root question (which pairs share a geometric motif) depends on sampling depth. With more compute we sample more poses per pair; if the motif is real, deep sampling reveals consistent3-fold engagement for competent pairs; if not, deeper sampling shows multiple conflicting poses (no shared motif).\n\nBut careful: \"predictions.if_lever and predictions.if_not are the outcomes you expect, on the same measured quantity, if more compute does or does not change the answer.\" So both predictions are on the same measured quantity. The decision rule says how the measured result decides between them.\n\nMeasured quantity candidate: \"stability of the motif assignment (3-fold-axis engagement) per pair between the medium and full compute levels\" — e.g., fraction of pairs with identical assignment. Or \"the number of pose models needed until the top interface cluster converges\".\n\nAlternative measured quantity: \"interface-cluster convergence\": at what number of sampled poses does the dominant binding-mode cluster stop changing. More compute (lever) means the answer (dominant motif) changes when we sample more; not lever means it's already converged at low levels.\n\nHmm wait. Let me think again about what \"the answer\" is. The answer = whether transcytosis-competent pairs share a geometric motif. More compute = more poses/pairs/seeds modeled. if compute is the lever: exhaustive enumeration changes the conclusion relative to the baseline (few runs) — e.g., at72 runs no clear motif, at 576 runs a clear shared motif emerges (or vice versa). if_not: conclusion at 72 runs equals conclusion at 576 runs.\n\nBut for a research plan, the more useful prediction is about what we'll find. Let me set:\n\nMeasured quantity: \"number of the 12 tested capsids whose classification as 3-fold-axis GPI-engaging (or not) is unchanged when pose sampling per pair doubles from 24 to 48 runs\" — that's a convergence measure.\n\nSimpler: measured quantity = \"the separation between transcytosis-competent and intracellular pairs in interface geometry (3-fold engagement score)\". Compute levels = total model runs. if_lever: at 576 runs the separation is ≥X and at 72 runs it was <X — compute changed the answer. if_not: separation at 72 and 576 agree within noise.\n\nLet me define the compute axis as \"capsid–receptor pose models computed (docking + AF-Multimer)\". Levels: 72, 288, 576 or similar. And the answer: whether a shared3-fold-axis motif marks transcytosis-competent pairs.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: the \"held_fixed\" and arms need to be concrete. Let me design the actual experiment.\n\n**What to build:**\n\nA pipeline that:\n1. Takes a fixed panel of capsid sequences/structures (engineered AAV library, e.g., 12 variants with known BBB transcytosis phenotypes: PHP.B/PHP.eB (LY6A-dependent transcytosis), AAV9 (weak), AAV2, AAV8, AAVrh.10, AAV-X1 (LY6A? actually AAV-X1 targets LY6A per Voyager), CAP-Mac (LRP6 peptide), etc.)\n2. Takes 6 receptor ectodomains (LY6A, LY6C1, ALPL, LRP6, CA4, AAVR)\n3. For each capsid × receptor pair: \n   a. Dock receptor ectodomain onto capsid surface (whole capsid or VP trimer patch) — e.g., HDOCK/ClusPro-style global docking → top20 poses\n   b. AF-Multimer (via ColabFold) on the capsid receptor-binding loop + receptor domain, 5 seeds × 5 models\n   c. MD refinement of top poses (GROMACS,20–50 ns) — maybe too expensive for all; only top cluster reps\n4. Analysis: map each pose onto capsid symmetry axes (2-fold, 3-fold, 5-fold); test whether transcytosis-competent pairs cluster at 3-fold axis with GPI-anchored receptors.\n\nTranscytosis phenotype labels come from literature (PHP.B→LY6A transcytosis is known; AAV9 → intracellular/low; etc.). The scored_against = published phenotype labels of capsid–receptor pairs (e.g., PHP.B/PHP.eB require LY6A for BBB transcytosis).\n\nBaseline: \"published modeling covers only single-digit complexes\" — baseline = the two LRP6 peptide complexes from PMC11381518, i.e., 2 complexes modeled. Baseline value = 2 complexes. Or baseline = what you get with minimal compute: one AF-Multimer run per pair without docking/MD.\n\nAnchor: reproduce a published AF-Multimer result — e.g., the LRP6/peptide complex from PMC11381518 (or its interface contacts) within RMSD tolerance. Need to fetch that paper to get a number (e.g., interface RMSD, or the peptide-binding site). If the paper gives pLDDT/PAE or contact residues, we can anchor to that.\n\nLet me now do the research. I'll fetch:\n1. PMC11381518\n2. Voyager poster PDF\n3. PMC12682079\n4. Lambda pricing page\n5. Maybe ColabFold/AF-Multimer runtime sources (e.g., ColabFold GitHub README benchmark, AF3 cluster docs)\n6. Search for AAV capsid-receptor structures (AAV2/AAVR structure? AAV9/LY6A?), PHP.B/LY6A structure.\n\nAlso need the pilot: \"Run every local command under timeout. Start no new local run 45 minutes after you begin.\" I could run a quick local pilot to estimate AF-Multimer or docking runtime — but no GPU locally presumably (4-core machine shared). A pilot could measure something like: parsing PDB files, computing capsid symmetry axis positions, or a simple docking score calculation with a small script. That would give MEASURED numbers. E.g., I could write a Python script that enumerates capsid×receptor pairs and computes the number of jobs, or compute the geometric placement of GPI-anchor attachment points on a capsid at 2/3/5-fold axes from a known AAV capsid structure (download from RCSB). But \"Read only pages you fetch and what you write under ...\" — I can fetch RCSB PDB files (webfetch) and then... hmm, but a local script would need to read the downloaded file. I can write files only under the idea dir. So I can fetch a PDB file and save it under the idea dir? The instruction: \"write only under .../55c57ced\". So downloads must go there. But do I have network access from the shell? Not sure. webfetch works. I could webfetch the PDB file and then write its content to a file under pilot/ and run a script on it. That could give MEASURED geometric numbers (e.g., distances from 3-fold axis to known receptor binding loops).\n\nBut careful with time: \"Start no new local run 45 minutes after you begin\". I should do the essential work first and keep any local runs short.\n\nActually, is a MEASURED number needed? The schema doesn't require MEASURED. But numbers that drive the cost need bases. MEASURED would be nice for per-job runtime, but I can't run AF-Multimer locally. I could MEASURE something small: e.g., the time to do an exhaustive pairwise geometry enumeration on a CPU, or the size of the job grid (DERIVED instead). A cheap MEASURED pilot: compute the capsid symmetry-axis geometry from a real AAV9 capsid structure — verify that GPI-anchor attachment points can be placed within X Å of the 3-fold axis. That is a real check of feasibility of the motif hypothesis. Let me consider.\n\nActually, a very useful pilot: download an AAV capsid structure (e.g., AAV9 VP3 or full capsid, PDB 3UX1 or similar), identify the 3-fold axis coordinates, compute distances from published receptor-binding residues (e.g., PHP.B LY6A-binding residues, AAVR-binding residues) to the 3-fold axis. If the binding sites are near the 3-fold axis, the motif hypothesis is geometrically plausible. That's a MEASURED number with a script and output under pilot/. Good.\n\nBut fetching a PDB via webfetch returns text I can save... webfetch returns content to me; then I write it with the write tool to pilot/. The write tool writes files — allowed under the idea dir. Then run a Python script with timeout. OK.\n\nHowever, PDB files can be huge (viral capsid = millions of atoms). Better to fetch a smaller structure: VP3 monomer of AAV9 (e.g., PDB 3UX1 is AAV9 VP3? Let me check via search). Or use the asymmetric unit. Hmm, maybe use an AlphaFold-predicted receptor or a capsid with known binding-site mutations.\n\nAlternatively the pilot can be simpler: run a small Python script that does \"exhaustive capsid–receptor complex enumeration\" geometry enumeration over idealized capsomer coordinates to measure wall time for the analysis stage and the number of pose bins. But that seems contrived.\n\nLet me think about what the cost needs. The cost lines need hours as expressions and rates. The key uncertain numbers:\n- per AF-Multimer job runtime on H100 (GUESS or PROXY from published benchmarks — ColabFold README states fold speed; AlphaFold-Multimer on ~500 residues takes ~10-20 min on V100 per some sources)\n- number of jobs: pairs × poses × seeds (DESIGN/DERIVED)\n- docking runtime (CPU/GPU)\n- MD refinement runtime per pose (PROXY from GROMACS benchmarks)\n\nSources to find:\n1. ColabFold README: \"on a RTX3090 ... 15 min\" type numbers. The ColabFold GitHub has benchmark tables.\n2. AlphaFold3 or AF2 paper supplementary: runtime per prediction.\n3. GROMACS benchmarks (e.g., JACS2021 or the GROMACS performance page) for ns/day on A100/H100.\n4. HDOCK/ClusPro: server-based, free — but for local compute, maybe use LightDock or pyDock. HDOCK web server is free (no compute cost). Actually docking could be done on Lambda CPUs (included in GPU instance vCPUs) or via free web servers — that would reduce cost. But web servers have queue limits. Better: do docking locally on the instance CPUs (included).\n\nLet me now think about realistic job sizes for AF-Multimer. The big issue: a full AAV capsid is 60-mer of ~530 aa VP3 each = way too big for AF-Multimer. The published approach (PMC11381518) models a peptide (from capsid VR loop) × LRP6 domain — small. The Voyager poster suggests \"3-fold-axis engagement of GPI-anchored receptors\" — GPI-anchored receptors (LY6A, LY6C1, ALPL, CA4) have small ectodomains (~100-130 aa).A sensible tractable unit: capsid variable-region peptide/loop (20–60 aa) + receptor ectodomain (100–150 aa) = 150–250 residues per complex. AF-Multimer on 250 residues: a few minutes per seed on H100. With 5 models × 5 seeds = 25 runs per pair... each ~2-5 min → ~1-2 GPU-hours per pair.\n\nPlus whole-capsid docking: use rigid-body docking of receptor ectodomain onto a whole-capsid surface (or VP trimer patch with symmetry constraints). Tools: HDOCK (CPU), ClusPro (server), RosettaDock (CPU), LightDock (CPU/GPU). This gives poses at various symmetry axes; we bin by axis (2-fold, 3-fold, 5-fold). Then AF-Multimer refines the interface region.\n\nMD refinement: GROMACS on ~50k atoms (capsid patch + receptor + membrane?) for 20 ns. On A100: maybe 50-200 ns/day for50k atoms → 20 ns is2-10 hours per pose. Too expensive to do for all poses; do for cluster representatives only (e.g., 2 per pair × 72 pairs = 144 MD runs × 3h = 432 GPU-hours = way over budget). Need to cut: MD only for the top pairs or only for motif validation (e.g., 12 runs).\n\nBudget math ($1,000):\n- Say 8× H100 at $3.99/GPU/h = $31.92/instance-h. $1,000 / $31.92 = 31.3 instance-hours = 250 GPU-hours. That's a lot of GPU-hours if fully utilized.\n- Or 8× A100 40GB at $1.99/GPU/h = $15.92/instance-h → 62.8 instance-hours = 502 GPU-hours.\n- AF-Multimer doesn't need H100; A100 is fine. ColabFold on A100 is well established.\n\nLet me design with A100 8× at $1.99 (per_gpu_usd_h) — wait, the price sheet: \"NVIDIA A100 SXM, 40GB, 8 GPUs, $1.99/GPU/h\". So 8× A100 40GB = $15.92/h.\n\nJob count design:\n- Capsid library: 12 capsids (DESIGN)\n- Receptors: 6- Pairs: 72 (DERIVED)\n- AF-Multimer per pair: 5 seeds × 1 model? or 5 seeds × 5 models? ColabFold default: num_models=5, num_recycles=3. Let me say 5 seeds × 5 models = 25 predictions per pair → 1800 predictions total. Too many? Each ~2-4 min on A100 for 250 residues → 1800 × 3 min = 5400 min = 90 GPU-hours. Fine within budget on 8 GPUs = 11 wall-hours.\n- Docking per pair: whole-capsid rigid docking, CPU: 30-60 min per pair on 8-16 cores → 72 h CPU total, spread over vCPUs (124-240 vCPUs) → few hours wall-clock. Basically free (included in instance price).\n- MD refinement: only for cluster representatives passing a gate. E.g., 24 MD runs × 20 ns × 3 GPU-h each = 72 GPU-hours.\n\nTotal GPU-hours ~ 160-200 → well within $1,000 on A100. Then the budget constraint is more about scope than dollars. But the task wants \"up to $1,000\" — so I should design a plan that spends meaningfully up to ~$1,000, with time boxes and stretch lines.\n\nHmm — but the plan should also test the compute lever: levels of compute (e.g., 24, 72, 288, 576 model runs). The \"levels\" of compute_axis.\n\nLet me reconsider the compute axis: quantity = \"capsid–receptor pose models computed\" (docking poses scored + AF-Multimer predictions). Levels could be [48, 192, 768] or in terms of pairs enumerated at increasing depth: [12 pairs × 4 seeds, 72 pairs × 5, 72 pairs × 20]. Hmm. For \"exhaustive enumeration\", the axis that more compute buys = number of (capsid, receptor, pose) model runs. Levels: e.g., 72 (one pose per pair), 360 (5 per pair), 1440 (20 per pair).\n\nWait — also the axis could be number of capsid library members. But the option is \"exhaustive enumeration\" over capsid × receptor × pose. The compute-open text: \"More parallel compute would buy model runs (capsid × receptor × pose)\". So quantity = \"model runs (capsid × receptor × pose)\". Unit: \"complex models\". Levels: maybe72, 360, 1800 (or 3 levels matching budget: pilot-scale, full-scale, stretch).\n\nPredictions on the same measured quantity... what's the measured outcome? Perhaps \"fraction of known-transcytosis pairs whose dominant pose cluster sits at the 3-fold axis\" (motif detection). With more runs (more poses), do we find the motif?\n\nif_lever (compute is the lever): at ≥1440 runs, a stable shared motif appears (e.g., ≥5 of 6 known competent pairs show 3-fold-axis GPI engagement, and this classification is stable under resampling), whereas at 72 runs the classification is unstable — compute changed the answer.\nif_not: classification at 72 runs is identical (within noise) to classification at 1440 runs — the answer doesn't depend on sampling depth; if no motif is found at any level, enumeration closes the catalog.\n\nHmm, but \"if_lever and if_not are the outcomes you expect, if more compute does or does not change the answer\" — so they're predictions of outcomes conditional on the lever being effective. The decision_rule distinguishes them with a threshold number. noise_rule: how to tell real difference from run-to-run noise (seeds, CI, paired evaluation).\n\nMeasured quantity should be a number: e.g., \"motif_assignment_stability\" = fraction of pairs whose3-fold-vs-other classification is unchanged between compute level N and level 2N; or \"motif separation score\" = (fraction of competent pairs at 3-fold) − (fraction of retained pairs at 3-fold).\n\nLet me pick: measured quantity = \"3-fold engagement gap\" = (share of transcytosis-competent pairs whose dominant receptor-binding pose lies within 15 Å of the capsid 3-fold axis) minus (share of intracellular pairs so placed). Threshold: gap ≥ 0.5 at full scale and classification stable (κ ≥ 0.8 across seeds).\n\nif_lever: \"At 1,440 model runs the competent-minus-retained 3-fold gap reaches ≥0.50, while at 72 runs it is below 0.25\" — compute changed the answer.\nif_not: \"The gap at 72 runs already matches 1,440 runs within ±0.15; deeper enumeration does not change which pairs share the motif.\"\n\ndecision_rule: run the full grid twice at two sampling depths (paired, same seeds); if |gap_deep − gap_shallow| ≥ 0.25 and both estimates' bootstrap95% CIs are disjoint → if_lever; else if_not.\n\nnoise_rule: 5 independent AF-Multimer seeds per pair; consensus classification per pair = majority of seeds; paired bootstrap over pairs (n=12 capsids × 6 receptors) for CI; run-to-run noise estimated from seed spread (if seed-to-seed classification agreement < 0.8, the pair is \"unresolved\" and excluded).\n\nOK. Now, scored_against: \"the published phenotype labels of capsid–receptor pairs — e.g., PHP.B/PHP.eB–LY6A transcytosis competence vs. AAV9–LY6A intracellular retention\" — compared with experimental literature (Voyager poster; LY6A dependency papers).\n\nNow the phases:\n\nP0 (setup/anchor): fetch structures, build inputs. Anchor: reproduce the published LRP6–peptide AF-Multimer interface from PMC11381518 (e.g., interface contacts or binding site residues) with our ColabFold setup within RMSD ≤ 2.5 Å / same contact set. Need to check the paper for an anchorable number.\n\nP1 (pilot/measure): time one AF-Multimer job, one docking job, one MD job on the target GPU; measure per-job wall time → per_job_h numbers (MEASURED). Gate: per AF-Multimer job ≤ some threshold, else adjust.\n\nP2 (enumerate): run the capsid × receptor × pose grid at shallow depth.\n\nP3 (deep sampling + MD refine): deeper seeds for convergence; MD refinement of cluster reps.\n\nP4 (analysis): symmetry-axis binning, motif test, bootstrap.\n\nNow restructuring: sequential = none (per option record), method = \"none\". But wait — the prompt says the schema's restructuring.method can be \"none\" if the plan only runs independent work side by side. Then compute_axis.quantity is the quantity varied. And I don't need condition/condition_phase/baseline_sequential/baseline_copies... but \"test_summary names both baselines\" — hmm: \"anchor checks the parallel result against the sequential one within a stated tolerance; test_summary names both baselines\". That phrasing is in the block about restructuring when method is not none. Hmm, re-read:\n\n\"If method is not none, also give condition, ... baseline_sequential, ... and baseline_copies, ... Then compute_axis is the length of the chain or the number of runs the answer needs; predictions...; anchor checks the parallel result against the sequential one within a stated tolerance; test_summary names both baselines; and novelty.closest_prior is the nearest published parallel version of this computation.\"\n\nThis \"Then compute_axis...\" paragraph seems to apply when method is not none. But compute_axis is always required. And \"test_summary names both baselines\" — if method is none, maybe there's just the one baseline. But to be safe, I can include baseline_sequential and baseline_copies in restructuring anyway (optional fields), and make test_summary mention baselines. Hmm, but if method is \"none\", including those fields might be contradictory (\"If method is not none, also give...\"). The safest: method = \"none\" since the option record explicitly says sequential is none, and omit the extra fields. But then \"test_summary names both baselines\" wouldn't apply... The instruction \"test_summary names both baselines\" is within the method-not-none paragraph. I'll keep test_summary naming the baseline (single-digits AF-Multimer complexes, minimal-depth enumeration) — and I can name two baselines anyway: (1) published single-digit complexes baseline (2 LRP6 peptide complexes), (2) minimal-compute baseline (one model per pair). That satisfies both readings.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually let me reconsider whether method should be \"none\". The computational structure: is there a sequential chain? Building structures → docking → AF-Multimer → MD → analysis. There IS a pipeline order: you need the complex before MD. But \"sequential_part is the part of this computation that one answer waits on step after step, and how many steps\" — for one pair's answer: dock → select poses → AF-Multimer → MD → classify. That's ~3-4 steps in a chain. The method: those steps could be computed at the same time... not really — MD needs the docked pose. But different pairs run in parallel, and within a pair, seeds run in parallel. The option record says sequential is \"none\". I'll follow the option record: method = \"none\", sequential_part describing that each pair is independent (with the note that per-pair refinement is short). Actually sequential_part card field max 18 words, plain words: \"None: every capsid–receptor pair is an independent docking and folding job; only four short refinement steps per pair.\" Hmm that's more than 18 words. \"None: each capsid–receptor pair is independent; only four short refinement steps chain within one pair.\" — 15 words. Good.\n\nNow, novelty: closest_prior = PMC11381518 (AF-Multimer of LRP6 with AAV-X1 and CAP-Mac VR-VIII peptides — 2 complexes).